> One scroll out of 23 scrolls gives pretty damned good odds. 

I play the game as though each character should try to ascend.  A one in
23 chance is not particularly high, but neither is it so small that
you won't see one.

If you're playing to win, rather than to an alternate victory condition,
then confuse-testing unIDed scrolls is bad play for more reasons than
just "you might find genocide".  But that one's good enough, and very
adequately demonstrates my original point, which is that the risk-reward
ratio for reading unIDed scrolls is tilted so heavily in the direction
of "risk" that the correct answer for a player is to -never- read unIDed
scrolls.  This is bad.

Ideally, the risk and reward should be approximately equal.  If there are 
high-risk scrolls, there should be high-reward scrolls.  ("scroll of
wishing" comes to mind, though it's obviously broken WRT magic markers)

> Part of the fun of playing NetHack is the fact that it actively works to 
